 \section{Introduction}
-A fixed per-image channel-symbol budget limits the source description available for image reconstruction. Digital coding and learned joint source--channel coding (JSCC) address this resource constraint through different source and channel representations~\cite{deepjscc,swinjscc}. With a generative receiver, transmitted information constrains reconstruction to the source image while a learned prior completes the untransmitted content. The resulting design problem is to retain useful source information within the frame and use it to preserve image structure and appearance.
+A fixed per-image channel-symbol budget must accommodate source information, signaling, and channel protection. Digital coding and learned joint source--channel coding (JSCC) address this constraint through different representations~\cite{deepjscc,swinjscc}. With a generative receiver, delivered information constrains reconstruction to the source image while a learned prior predicts untransmitted content. Efficient communication depends on both retaining useful information within the frame and using it during reconstruction.
 
-Next-scale representations organize images into complete coarse token maps followed by residual refinements. After resizing, each coarse map covers the full latent grid; finer maps add residual detail. Visual autoregressive modeling (VAR) reported state-of-the-art results on its original class-conditional ImageNet generation benchmarks~\cite{var}, motivating its use as a completion prior. ARPC uses next-scale token maps for progressive image compression and generates unreceived scales~\cite{arpc}. Ada-TokenCom couples next-token prefix coding and tail generation with wireless resource adaptation~\cite{adatokencom}, while TONIC addresses task-oriented token transmission~\cite{tonic}. We study how a next-scale residual description can fill each fixed digital frame when its capacity ends inside a scale.
+Next-scale tokenization organizes an image into complete coarse maps followed by residual refinements. Each resized coarse map covers the full latent grid, providing a basis for finer-scale prediction. Visual autoregressive modeling (VAR) reported state-of-the-art class-conditional ImageNet generation results~\cite{var}, motivating its use as a completion prior. ARPC uses next-scale entropy coding and scale completion for progressive image compression~\cite{arpc}. Ada-TokenCom couples next-token prefix coding and tail generation with wireless resource adaptation~\cite{adatokencom}; TONIC studies task-oriented token transmission~\cite{tonic}. We develop next-scale image communication under a hard symbol budget for each image.
 
-Adapting progressive descriptions to channel resources is a classical communication problem~\cite{chande}. For next-scale tokens, whole-scale boundaries need not coincide with fixed frame capacity: adding a scale requires many tokens, and the terminated entropy-code length varies across images. A frame may therefore have room for part of the next scale even when the complete scale does not fit. Within-scale endpoints can retain this otherwise excluded source information. The receiver must then incorporate the delivered boundary tokens into both the residual representation and the context used to predict finer scales.
+Adapting progressive descriptions to channel resources is a classical communication problem~\cite{chande}. Next-scale boundaries can leave usable frame capacity: a complete map may not fit although a subset of its tokens would. Entropy-code lengths vary across images, so fitting requires actual decodable lengths. The resulting incomplete boundary map must then contribute to residual reconstruction and to the conditions for predicting finer scales.
 
